        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>
Astronauts are exposed to weightlessness during spaceflight. To combat the muscle and bone loss that occurs as a result, astronauts spend a substantial amount of time exercising. Recently, zoledronate, an antiresorptive drug normally prescribed for osteoporotic patients, has been tested in astronauts (during spaceflight missions up to 6 months) with effective results of preventing bone structural losses. However, the effects of this pharmaceutical countermeasure on bone formation, damage repair, and bone quality are not fully known, especially in conditions of mechanical overload like resistance exercise.
<P>
This proposal poses two principal questions: First, what are the effects of antiresorptive countermeasures combined with high-force exercise on bone remodeling and overall bone health during simulated spaceflight? Second, as pilot studies, does epigenetics play a role in the mechanical regulation of bone remodeling during simulated-spaceflight? The term epigenetics describes changes in gene transcription that can affect biological adaptation to an environment--in this case, spaceflight--without changes to the sequence of DNA. Epigenetic processes are thought to underlie the process of cellular differentiation, where transcription of specific genes occurs in response to key stimuli, and may be heritable, thus, potentially affecting evolution in future generations. We hypothesize that the mechanical environment during spaceflight, namely microgravity-induced weightlessness, as well as exercise, regulate gene expression in the osteoblast-lineage cells both to control bone formation by osteoblasts and bone resorption by osteoclasts, which continually shapes bone structure throughout life.
<P>
This proposal has the following Specific Aims and Hypotheses: 
1) Determine the effects of combined mechanical loading and zoledronate on skeletal structure and bone remodeling during simulated weightlessness. 
2) Demonstrate whether the methylation status of key bone remodeling gene promoters are modulated in response to various levels of mechanical loading during simulated spaceflight. 
This proposal addresses multiple Research Recommendations for Animal Experiments and Gaps in Knowledge stated in the National Academy of Sciences Decadal Survey, Recapturing Space: Life and Physical Sciences for a New Era of Exploration. 
<P>
APPROACH:<br>To address Aim 1, the following three-pronged approach is taken. First, in order to simulate long-duration spaceflight, mice will be hindlimb unloaded (HU) with controls normally ambulating (NL). To prevent bone loss during simulated spaceflight, mice will be treated with the antiresorptive zolendronate or saline. Further, a third treatment will be introduced to each mouse; to simulate the effects of exercise, the right tibia will be loaded in axial compression, while the left tibia receive no additional treatment. This models astronauts exercising while taking an antiresorptive and will facilitate comparisons between HU and NL for indices of bone formation, structure, and quality.
<P>
To address Aim 2, we plan to take a two-pronged approach, using HU as a model of reduced mechanical loading and axial compression of the tibia as a model of increased mechanical loading. Thus, we are pushing the mechanostat in opposite directions to engage potential epigenetic mechanisms. First, the methylation status of key gene promoters will be observed in skeletal tissue (which contains terminally differentiated bone cells including embedded osteocytes, osteoblast lining cells, osteoclasts, and quiescent lining cells). Second, the methylation status of key gene promoters will be measured in osteoprogenitors cultured ex vivo from the marrow with or without the addition of a methyltransferase inhibitor. This approach allows both investigation of fundamental mechanisms that regulate transcription in bone cells during simulated spaceflight and comparisons between osteoblast-lineage cells in early and late stages of differentiation. Further, methylation results will be correlated with the histomorphometric results from Aim 1 to establish links between bone remodeling and methylation state.
<P>
RESULTS:<br>
This experiment is currently in progress.  Results will be available at the conclusion of the study.
